In article <C9BzIn.3Cq@mailer.cc.fsu.edu>, books@mailer.cc.fsu.edu (Roger Books) writes:
|>I need to increase the swap partition size on our new alpha running
|>OSF-1.  Just checking to see if the rule of thumb of 3x - 4x RAM size
|>is still appropriate.

it depends on alot of things.  your mileage may vary.

first, how much memory do you have?  the greater the memory, the smaller the
n times.  for less than 128MB, 3-4 times is probably a good rule of thumb.

secondly, are you using eager or lazy swap?  you need more space for eager
(which is required for POSIX compliance and the default algorithm), less for
eager.  above 128MB, you could probably get away with 2-2.5 times memory.

thirdly, depends on what you're running.  the hungrier your programs, the
larger the swap should be.

finally, you'll see improved swapping/memory allocation on the new version of 
OSF/1, coming out this summer, so after you upgrade, you might need less.

hope this helps.

g.
