OK, dhrystone and whetstone are freely available.  That's one advantage they
have over SPEC.  What are some of the "many" other ways they're better?

>You can run then on your own [computer].

Benchmarks like SPEC, LINPACK, dhrystone, et al, are for people who don't have
physical access to the boxes.  If they did, they'd run their own code and get
the real answer, not just an appromixation of a hint of what they might be able 
to predict for their own code.

>Spec programs are ideal benchmarks in almost all ways. Spec itself would be
>the ideal if it were not proprietary. What is needed is real-size
>benchmarks that are public domain.

All of the code in CINT and CFP is freely available.  What's proprietary is
SPEC's bundling of this code, including scripts and input files.  It ought to
be pretty easy to come up with a similar free packaging of the same programs
with similar but different test data, if one really thinks it's worthwhile.

>I simply don't trust results that I can't get from a list to which random 
>people with actual production machines can submit results. Vendors,
>by themselves, will cheat and do cheat. 

Given the choice between a list of random results by random people on random
benchmarks, and the published SPEC results run by the vendors, I'll take the
latter every time.  (Well, really I'll take both :-)  Any "cheating" the
vendors do on SPEC benchmarks is at least visible if you bother to look at the
published summaries (e.g., KAP and the matrix300 fiasco).  The embarassment
that would result if a vendor was really caught cheating makes it extremely
unlikely to ever happen.  And there are enough people out there with SPEC
source code that cheaters *would* be caught. 

