In article <C9G6rK.5FH@odin.corp.sgi.com>, mash@mash.wpd.sgi.com (John R. Mashey) writes:
|>I think I've explained in the past how difficult it was to get benchmarks that:
|> [...]
|>	e) Were checkable, either by having identical results, or for some
|>	FP codes, identical within reasonable tolerances.  Some otherwise
|>	wonderful benchmarks got killed by this requirement.  For example,
|>	timberwolf is a mostly integer program that does ECAD chip layout
|>	using simulated annealing.  It's usefully large, ported easily to
|>	all platfroms, and is *useful* on all the platfroms.  We loved it,
|>	except that on 12 platforms, it produced 3 distinct answers, with
|>	slightly different iteration counts.  (The "answer" is a chip layout,
|>	and all 3 solutions were feasible, and useful, and about the same
|>	size.)  We think this came from doing an occasional FP comparison,
|>	and then making a yes/no decision upon the result, and we never
|>	could cosntruct a version without this property.  ARGH!

There are many numerical codes around with this property. That is,
different machine arithmetics produce different output.  Sometimes slightly
different, sometimes totally different.  In the latter case, usually,
any answer is equivalent to the others and considered "good".

As an example, programs following the motion of a set of particles
by integrating some equations of motion, very common in condensed
matter physics, chemistry, astrophysics, etc, are of such kind.
When there is a difference, even if tiny, between two trajectories,
this difference is going to explode exponentially as time goes on.
And who cares.  No one is worried about where exactly the final phase 
space point is, because the programs are used to compute time averages.

And in your example, all machines produce a good chip layout by
simulated annealing (a stochastic technique). So...that's fine! 
Why the heck you do not consider these programs to be valid
benchmarks??  When an effort is made to stabilize the number
of operations (for example by replacing a convergence criterion
with a fixed number of iterations, and that is not usually difficult),
the program (IMHO) should be an acceptable benchmark regardless of 
the actual flow of numbers in the execution.
And authors of the program should have little trouble in providing
an acceptance criterion, saying whether the results are to be
considered good or not. 

Just my 0.02c pragmatic view.

