  I posted this question last week and received several replies, but
none really solved the problem.  I have an Apple Laserwriter IIf
connected via serial line to a DEC 3000/400.  This is an extremely
similar setup to the one we had with a DECstation 5000/200 which the Alpha
just replaced.  The problem is that large, multiple page postscript 
files  (around 300Kbytes or more) fail to print properly.  They get 
truncated at a random point.  Sometimes all of the pages of the plot 
will make it through.  Most of the time, only one or two pages make it.
The postscript files are clean.  They preview fine and print without a
problem on a printer connected via LAT to the Alpha.
  I've tried many different permutations of flag bits in the printcap file.
I've also tried different print filters including psflpr as suggested by 
several people.  However, the problem remains.  For reference, my
printcap entry is:

apple2|lp4|Apple Laserwriter Plus on kelvin:\
        :br#19200:\
        :ct=dev:\
        :fc#0177777:\
        :fs#03:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lp4err:\
        :lp=/dev/tty01:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/ln03rof:\
        :pl#66:\
        :pw#80:\
        :rw:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/apple2:\
        :sf:\
        :sh:\
        :xc#0177777:\
        :xs#044000:

  With the Adobe error handler (ehandler.ps) installed on the printer,
I see messages like:
  ERROR: undefined
  OFFENDING COMMAND: ...
which occur at different points in the same file.  I've tried connecting
the printer to other identically configured DEC 3000/400's and the errors
continue to occur.  Looking at the print output on a terminal connected
to the serial line shows things looking okay.
  I get the impression that maybe some noise is somehow getting into
the serial lines and messing things up.  Alternately, perhaps the Apple
is having problems handling communication with the Alpha.  I've tried
slower baud rates and the errors remain.
  I'm stumped at this point.  Is nobody else out there experiencing
difficulties like this for printers connected to the AXP's serial lines?
This looks like a reproducible problem, not unique to my machine.
The printer is okay; it's worked fine up until the time that the Alpha
replaced the DECstation last week.
  Any ideas would be appreciated.

Richard Eckman
NASA Langley
eckman@eos1.larc.nasa.gov


