>In article <1993Jun22.144348.26782@ryn.mro4.dec.com>, bhandarkar@msbcs.enet.dec.com (Dileep Bhandarkar) writes:
>|> One difference to note is that HP bundles the "KAP" preprocessor with their
>|> compiler; it has to be licensed separately and installed on AXP systems. 
>|> KAP makes a significant difference on some benchmarks.
>
>Yes, I had requested KAP on our quote from DEC, but the demo machine we 
>received did not have it and thus I was unable to test it.  It is my 
>understanding that the KAP preprocessor on HP is invoked with the "+OPn" 
>switch.  I didn't use this because I thought it would be an unfair comparison
>vs. the DEC without the KAP.
>
>
>|> There is a wide variation within CFP92. There are 2:1 ratios in both 
>|> directions.  A different benchmark may yield different results.
>
>Actually, CFP92 was the first benchmark we looked at, but our local DEC rep
>faxed us 30 pages worth of benchmarks.  Except for the 100x100 LINPACK (where
>the HP and 500X were virtually identical), the 500X was faster than the 735 
>in *every* case.
>
>These benchmarks are (I may have a couple of figures wrong since the Fax was fuzzy):
>  - SPEC CINT92 (Alpha 37% faster)
>  - SPEC CFP92  (Alpha 10% faster)
>  - SPECrate_int92    (Alpha 43% faster)
>  - SPECrate_fp92     (Alpha 30% faster)
>  - Linpack 1000x1000 (Alpha 24% faster)
>  - DN&R Labs CPU2 Benchmark    (Alpha 53% faster)
>  - AIM Suite III     (Alpha 54% higher performance rating)
>  - CERN Benchmark    (Alpha 10% faster)
>
>Now, based on the above (seemingly comprehensive) benchmarks, the 500X should 
>*at least* give the same performance as the 735.  Yet, I have run at least 8
>different codes (real life codes used here) that all out perform the 500 and, 
>scaling for clock speeds to the 500X, out perform the 500X too:
>
>  - Reactor acoustic analysis code (HP 2.5 times faster than Alpha 500)
>  - Crack Formation calculation code (HP 1.6 times faster)
>  - Neutrino Calculation code (HP 1.9 times faster)
>  - Health Physics code Case A (HP 5.5 times faster)
>  - Health Physics code Case B (HP 3.9 times faster)
>  - Groundwater simulation (HP 3.2 times faster)
>
>(Yes, these are vague descriptions, but I wasn't in contact with the authors
>directly.  These are however, real life codes.)
>
>Now, these numbers are not meant to incite a flame war, I just need to
>give the entire picture of what I'm finding.  I have corresponded with
>a few DEC people and they agree with what compiler switches I am using,
>but they are suggesting profiling.
>
>This is *exactly* my point.  Just by using regular compiler switches,
>I can't reproduce the seemingly resounding defeat of HP by DEC.
>

As noted, if you look at the detailed CFP92 results you will see 2:1 ratios
in both directions.  As for the DEC rep showing benchmarks which all favor
the DEC machine, this is hardly surprising.  Ask the HP rep for some
benchmarks, and I expect he/she could give you an equally long list
which favor the HP machine.  You don't really expect a salescritter to
give the competition ammunition do you?  If you want a balanced comparison,
get information from both sides.  Don't depend on either side for accurate
info about the other.  In a previous job, I was similarly naive, and
ended up buying and living with an inferior product because of it.  Not an
experience I care to repeat.

By the way, it might be true that the KAP preprocessor has a greater
benefit for one workstation than another either due to specifics of the
architecture or due to the compiler group at HP relying on the KAP
preprocessor to do certain optimizations since it is available with
all machines, while the DEC compiler group can't make that assumption.
I don't know of any specific examples, but I would not assume that omitting
the preprocessor from both products is the same as applying the preprocessor
to both products.

I'm having trouble understanding your position about profiling.  If you
really want to understand the fundamental reasons why one machine is
better than another, you need to investigate.  Profiling is a fundamental
tool for performance investigation.  Once you identify that 100 lines of
code where 90% of the execution time is occurring, a DEC or HP expert
can say "oh, you are thrashing the cache, or our compiler is spilling
registers, but in the next release we'll have it fixed, or whatever".
Perhaps you will learn about that magic compiler switches that can be
applied for all your codes, but which is not appropriate in some contexts
[i.e. trade more compile time for less execution time].  If you are not
willing to profile, you can't expect the performance analysts to be
able to help you investigate what your issues are.


>Thus, my conclusions are not "DEC is cheating."  They are not "SPEC benchmarks
>are useless."  BUT, my conclusions are "What the hell is going on here??"
>

A fair question.
Unfortunately, with workstations moving into rarified levels of performance,
they are becoming subject to extreme variances in effective rates due to
small changes in program environment.  Falling out of cache can often
cost you a factor of 5 in performance.


