I've added a test I performed on an Alpha 3000/500. Note that firstly I used
the c89 compiler (I think this gives slightly better results). The cc compiler
can handle -O3 if you statically link (-non_shared). This is documented in the
release notes. Also, I defined long to be integer. Ok this is somewhat dubious
in many cases, but in this particular case happens to be ok (no apparent
clashes with system calls requiring type long). The thinking behind this is
that the cache is only a specific size and you can fit twice as many integers
in there as you can longs. And secondly that most of the other machines we're
comparing against have sizeof(int) == sizeof(long). (I know nothing about the
HP machines though and this is where the test is important.) Do people feel
this is a 'fair' thing to do in a test? (Of course, vendors don't play 'fair'
in such things :-).

We had a similar piece of DNA alignment code I used to test our Alpha with
when we first obtained one. Curiously the SGI R4000 based machines outstripped
it quite substantially. DEC however took the code and did a bit of quick
analysis and returned it with a few changes yielding a significant (upto
double) speed increase on the Alpha, yet with only a marginal increase on the
SGI machine. Their particular consisting of moving frequently accessed global
variables to local variables to aid the compiler in register allocation
and secondly reordering 4 arrays into a single array of structure (and thus
greatly increasing the efficiency of the cache).

It seems from this that obviously certain programs will perform very well and
others will perform badly. My personal conclusion was that the existing alpha
compilers cannot handle the badly structured programs as well as many other
compilers.

I've tried gcc on the Alpha too - but it (the alpha assembler actually)
complains about the large local array declared in main. I guess the native
alpha compilers do some cheats to resolve this issue.

	James


