Comparing sim and LINPACK results are comparing apples and oranges.  The
sim program is an INTEGER (emphasis added) intensive program while
LINPACK is a FLOATING POINT (again) intensive program.  The HP PA-RISC
and the IBM systems are tuned for high floating point performance, and
lesser integer performance, while the new SUN 10/31, 10/40, etc and the
MIPS R4000 are of more equal integer and floating point performance. 
This is easily shown in the SPEC92 results for the architectures.  It
has been my experience, on all 4 of these platforms, that the SUN and
SGI/MIPS machines show an even performance across all of my
applications, while the IBM and HP have particular ones that they do
very well on (not at the same time) and others that they are not as fond
of.  Overall, for a mix of applications, it was a wash between an HP
720, IBM 550, SUN 10/41 and a R4000.  

At the risk of repeating myself, my INDIGO R4000, got the following
results:

     real   349.0    user 336.2    sys 3.5   (cc -O3 -mips2)

using IRIX 4.0.5 and the SGI cc 3.10.   That is faster than the ALPHA
and not too far behind the HP735.   Of course, the ALPHA and the HP blow
it away on the MFLOPS and LINPACK, but on real apps (at least mine) they
are not so far apart.


