Alot of people are asking for my codes to try.  I was under the impression
that all of them were proprietary.  It turns out that one of them isn't 
(I just spoke with the person who ran it)

The program is "mcnp4" from Los Alamos, and I am told that it is used by
every research nuclear site in the world.  (Another interesting
thing--apparently it dates back to the Manhattan Project and represents
about 400 man-years of effort since then!)

This code is about 45000 lines of code and (I am told) is a pathological
case with respect to cache hits.

Anyway, the code run on our data file gave:

Alpha 400: 16 minutes
Alpha 500: 15 minutes
HP 735   : 10.5 minutes

If this code is as prevalent as I am told, then:

a) DEC should beable to get ahold of this to run, tune, profile, whatever....
b) Other people on the net may beable to get some comparisons with HP
   and Alpha
c) People won't think I'm hiding behind "Our code is proprietary--so you
   can't have it to rebut our postings" 

One interesting thing above:  (This is just an observation, no conclusive
results.)  The best case for the Alpha 500X is presumably 11.25 minutes 
(based on the 150 to 200 MHz clock comparison).  However, using that same
argument, the 500 should be 14.2 minutes.

Anyway, my 2 cents.

Disclaimer: I speak for myself, not AECL Research.
            These are not "be all and end all benchmarks".  They are meant
            to initiate discussion on the net that will benefit all
            users and DEC and HP hardware.

            I invite people to show specific cases where the Alpha is faster.
