In article <20d2e7$845@sol.TIS.COM> mjr@tis.com (Marcus J Ranum) writes:
>[...], but if you look at the
>before and after pictures, you'll see that HP and IBM suddenly
>picked up something like a 200% improvement on ONE floating point
>benchmark. That doesn't usually indicate that someone came up
>with a clever general optimization that's going to make most
>people's code run faster; that indicates that someone cracked
