It is a very clear indication that the "benchmark" was not a real
application, and SPEC got bit by this one.  

MATRIX300 has two characteristics that make it a bad benchmark code.
First, it has a single hot spot -- indeed, the single hot spot is the
entire code.  Second, the hot spot was coded in a strikingly poor
manner.  People who do numerical linear algebra have known for at least
a decade that the coding in MATRIX300 is a dumb way to do matrix
multiplication.  In fact, MATRIX300 goes out of its way in being dumb,
by doing a matrix multiplication using a subroutine SGEMM, which does
the same thing as the standard BLAS3 routine, but with a different
calling sequence.  The obvious way of writing matrix multiply as a
triply nested loop would have been much more efficient on most machines,
as well as significantly shorter!  Instead, the MATRIX300 code writes
the Matrix-Matrix product (SGEMM) as a loop of calls to a Matrix-Vector
product subroutine (SGEMV), which is in turn written as a loop of calls
to a vector-vector product subroutine (SAXPY).   Structured programming
at its silliest?

If MATRIX300 were a real application, it would have taken all of 10
minutes to replace the SGEMM call with a call to the vendor's
hand-optimized matrix multiply code, and the entire code would be
reduced to a series of calls to the vendors library routine.  The code
that is present in MATRIX300 should be considered as a "last-resort"
hacked-together code for portability to those systems which do not have
a vendor-supplied matrix multiplication routine.

The performance of the vendors hand-coded matrix multiplication routine
is not a very interesting benchmark by itself because it provides
essentially no information that is not already provided by the LINPACK
1000x1000 results.  (The performance characteristics of Gaussian
elimination and matrix multiply are essentially identical on
uniprocessors, though the matrix multiply is perhaps a bit easier to
code).
