dbernard@thewho.Central.Sun.COM writes in response to me:
>>	The SPEC cooperative lost all credibility when they didn't
>> cry "FOUL!" over KAP. What a damn shame.
>
>	But isn't that what they, in effect, quietly did by coming out
>	with SPECint92 and SPECfp92, and recently declining to do any
>	more SPEC89ing?

	Yep, that's exactly what they did. But the word "quietly" in
your sentence sums it up.

	I used to work in customer support. I ran across lots of folks
who would buy machines based on MIPS or SPECmarks. Whichever vendor
sold the most MIPS/SPECs for a dollar. Now, you and I know that's
stupid, and that such individuals are the natural prey of sleazy
sales people. It was just a bit of a rude awakening for me to see
a lot of respected major vendors playing that game. I was also a bit
surprised that the technical community didn't raise a squawk.

	With respect to the other posters' comments on the ease of
optimizing MATRIX and how it was easy to fit - that's the point. A
benchmark isn't *supposed* to be fixed. I mean, if you run the
mjr benchmark

main() { while(1); }

	and optimize it so it's not as stupid, then it's not the same
benchmark. If I write a neat pre-processor that optimizes a benchmark's
source, that's benchmark specific, generally.

	What if I wrote a shell script that rearranged the loops in
MATRIX so it was better and included that as a "preprocessor" with
my compiler suite? Is that OK?

mjr.


