The system boots in multi-user if the environment variable BOOT_OSFLAGS
is set to "A".  If it is set to "" then you get single user which is the
default.  So either setenv BOOT_OSFLAGS "" at the >>> prompt or use
"b -fl" to temporarily ignore the setting of BOOT_OSFLAGS.  

Once you get your Docs online check section 2.3 of the "Guide to System
Administration".

As far as I know the only way to get single user from a running system
once you are actually logged in is "init s".

Until I read your post and was spurred to go and read the Docs I used to
CTRL-C during fsck to get it to abort and give me single user...


Ciao,							Marco

