In article <1993Jun14.083938.9893@daimi.aau.dk> bjornrey@daimi.aau.dk (Bj|rn Reynisson) writes:
> levanon@browneyes.ucs.indiana.edu (Jacob Levanon) writes:
> 
> >Greetings:
> >  We are trying to test the "user capacity" limits on an AXP 3000/400
> >running OSF1 V1.2 with 96Mg of memory configured with 138Mg of swap space in
> >one partition.  Our license "allows" unlimited number of users.  
> >The test was conducted by running a script that emulates users' logins
> >from a remote host.  The problem we ran into is that having logged in
> >the 28th user, the machine reports "its out of swap space..." and does
> >not allow any more logins.
> 
> >It seems to me that 138Mg of swap for a machine doing nothing else
> >should be sufficient to allow at least 32 users...  Is it a reasonable
> >expectation, or is it a known problem with this version of DEC's OSF1?
> 
> >Will greatly appreciate any comments/insights on the issue.  Has anyone
> >out there conducted a similar test?
> 
> >Thanks.
> > /====================================================================\
> >|| Jacob Levanon                   /\            Network Applications ||
> >|| University Computing Services   ||             levanon@indiana.edu ||
> >|| Indiana University              ||                  (812) 855-2598 ||
> >|| 750 N. State Road 46-Bypass     ||  ...reserved for                ||
> >|| Bloomington, IN  47405          \/                   future use... ||
> > \====================================================================/
> 
> Under DEC OSF/1 v1.2 you can allocate virtual memory using 1 of 2 policies:
> 
> 1) Eager evaluation - reserves space at each tasks creation.
> 
> 2) Lazy evaluation - reserves space on an as-needed basis.
> 
> Policy #1 is the default, but policy #2 can be activated by removing/renaming
> a file named /sbin/swapdefault .
> 
> Maybe this will solve your `out of swap space` problem.
> I have this information from 'Ultrix to DEC OSF/1 Technical Differences
> for System and Network Managers. Student Workbook'
> Order Number: EY-N433E-SG-0001   from Digital Equipment Corporation.
> 
> Please note that I have not tried this method, nor do I know if
> you have to reboot your system to activate the change.
> 
> ----------------------------------------------------------
> Bjorn Reynisson.

I have tried this and it works fine.  The machine is substantially
more usable than before.  I think eager allocation of swap is supposed
to be used in mission-critical database type environments which can
afford loads of disk space...  Lazy is much better for almost anything
else, at least under OSF which seems to declare enormous virtual sizes.

You do have to reboot after the change. 

However there is a possible gotcha.  When I did it, upon boot it it
complained about a /paging file that was missing and proceeded to WIPE
my /etc/rc.config file.  Given that this was my alpha server, I was not
amused.  It came back up claiming it was called "unknown".  I
reconstructed rc.config and repeated the procedure and it was fine.  It
is running quite happily now.  I think there was something missing
originally in my /etc/rc.config and it got confused when it came back.
Since our version of the full release arrived without release notes
maybe I missed something in the early installation.  Either way I would
make a copy of /etc/rc.config just in case.

Lastly to monitor swap usage use 

swapon -s

-s is a [Digital only] option.  Sounds like they forgot about pstat and
stuck it onto swapon at the last moment.  I feel that DEC should have
added a pointer man page for pstat to swapon -s.  


Ciao,							Marco


