I'm posting this to comp.sys.hp and comp.lang.fortran since there is some
content that readers of those groups may be interested in.


Many thanks to those who responded regarding my question on performance for
FORTRAN codes under OSF1 on Alpha.  (I was pleasantly surprised to get
a call from someone at DEC who follows this newsgroup.)  First, I'm
benchmarking an Alpha 500 but I'm looking to purchase a 500X (our local 
DEC folks couldn't muster up a 500X for us).  What I'm doing is taking 
the time required to run on the 500 and scaling by their relative clock 
speeds.  A few people have pointed out that this will give an optimistic
estimate of the 500X performance, but unfortunately it's the only thing 
I have to go on.  

Now for a few comments on the followup posts:

marc@synergy.Synergytics.Com (Marc Evans) wrote:


> There are a number of the aspects of the system that can be
> tuned, as well as multiple versions of some library functions
> which give different performance results. 

Does this have anything to do with " -assume noaccuracy_sensitive"?  Or
are there different libraries.  I know that SGI has a different set
of libraries that you can use if you don't need the last 3'ish
digits of precision.

> Yes, DEC can back up
> the numbers that they claim, and yes, they use the same bag of
> tricks that many other vendors use to get as high a performance
> spec as possible.

I have no doubt that they can backup their claim.  However, what I find
interesting is that I have an HP 735 and an Alpha 500, both of whose compilers
I have no experience with.  I sat down and browsed the manual pages on each
machine and picked what I thought were the most appropriate switches.  Then
I ran my code and showed the HP twice as fast as the Alpha.  Sure I can
tune the code, profile the code, etc. etc.  But I could do the same thing
for the HP, which would presumably boost its performance as well.  Even
if the tuned code on the Alpha goes *faster* than the HP, I don't have the
expertise or the time to tune all the codes I want to run.  For me, HP
gets top marks for running our codes fast *without* any tuning.

> Without more details of your benchmark, I can't really guess as
> to what specific tuning or alternate libraries would be of
> benefit to you. If you would please post more details, there are
> a number of people that read this group that can help you get
> the best possible performance from your environment.

Our codes (we've run a few and are getting the same slow performance on all)
are thermalhydraulic simulations of reactors and range from 10000 lines
of source to 25000 lines of source.  They iterate through space and time
to predict reactor transients.  I'm not sure what other characteristics
of the codes are important; I do know that the code I am interested in
doesn't use alot of transcendental functions.  It's almost entirely
expressions involving +,-,*,/.

rjones@pa.dec.com (Russ Jones) wrote: 

> The Digital techie may have been referring to the Digital Extended Math 
> Library for DEC OSF/1 AXP...

> "The Digital Extended Math Library (DXML) V2.0 is a set of libraries of 
> mathematical subroutines that are optimized for Digital platforms. Four 
> libraries are included covering the areas of Basic Linear Algebra (BLAS), 
> Linear System and Eigenproblem Solvers, Sparse Linear System Solvers, and 
> Signal Processing.

We're not using any external math routines.  Just the basic intrinsics.

kevinmac@ll.mit.edu (Kevin McElearney) wrote:

> I am running a 3000/400 with OSF 1.2 (10).  If I run jobs which do not need
> heavy I/O to the disk (including swapping) I get very good performance,
> especially graphics.  Once I access the local drive the performance quickly
> falls.  This happens on EVERY compile using my local disk and not NFS.

Right now my benchmarks are running entirely in memory and thus swapping
is not an issue.


Now for the summary of my original problem:

|> A techie at DEC said, "Oh, are you using the *old* IEEE math routines?  Those
|> give a major performance hit about an order of magnitude."  Now, disregarding
|> the order of magnitude claim (Computer sales people tend to forget that means
|> TEN TIMES FASTER :-), is there any substance to this?  Are there some *new*
|> math routines?

Yes, there is a new libm.a  Apparently there is a logical reversal regarding
exception handling in the sqrt() and exp() routines.  My performance results
above were obtained using this new math library.  One person who responded
to me showed a 300% performance improvement using the new library.  I 
unfortunately only showed a 20% performance improvement for my codes.

One more remark on this code tuning issue:  Published benchmarks are
presumably run with full optimization, profiling, etc. by all vendors.
Before I took on this benchmarking task, I used to believe that these
industry standard benchmarks are a good indication of *relative* performance,
but for close results running your own code is the best indication
of how the machines will perform in your environment.  Thus, when I 
saw the SPECfp92's for Alpha 500X and HP 735 within 10% of each other,
I thought it would be good to try our own code.  What I was not expecting
is to find the 735 twice as fast as the 500, and thus an estimated 50%
faster than the 500X!

So, are these benchmarks showing how good the *machines* are, or the *people*
who know their machines inside and out and thus can squeeze every bit 
of performance out of them?

Who cares how fast the machines are in these super optimal, hand-optimized,
dust-free environments??  I want speed in *my* environment.  And right now,
I'm not getting the performance DEC claims.  

Until there are more developments with Alpha, chalk one up for HP.

