In article <1993Jun22.144348.26782@ryn.mro4.dec.com>, bhandarkar@msbcs.enet.dec.com (Dileep Bhandarkar) writes:
|> One difference to note is that HP bundles the "KAP" preprocessor with their
|> compiler; it has to be licensed separately and installed on AXP systems. KAP
|> makes a significant difference on some benchmarks.

Yes, I had requested KAP on our quote from DEC, but the demo machine we received did not
have it and thus I was unable to test it.  It is my understanding that the KAP 
preprocessor on HP is invoked with the "+OPn" switch.  I didn't use this 
because I thought it would be an unfair comparison vs. the DEC without
the KAP.


|> There is a wide variation within CFP92. There are 2:1 ratios in both directions.
|> A different benchmark may yield different results.

Actually, CFP92 was the first benchmark we looked at, but our local DEC rep faxed
us 30 pages worth of benchmarks.  Except for the 100x100 LINPACK (where the HP
and 500X were virtually identical), the 500X was faster than the 735 in *every*
case.

These benchmarks are (I may have a couple of figures wrong since the Fax was fuzzy):
  - SPEC CINT92 (Alpha 37% faster)
  - SPEC CFP92  (Alpha 10% faster)
  - SPECrate_int92    (Alpha 43% faster)
  - SPECrate_fp92     (Alpha 30% faster)
  - Linpack 1000x1000 (Alpha 24% faster)
  - DN&R Labs CPU2 Benchmark    (Alpha 53% faster)
  - AIM Suite III     (Alpha 54% higher performance rating)
  - CERN Benchmark    (Alpha 10% faster)

Now, based on the above (seemingly comprehensive) benchmarks, the 500X should 
*at least* give the same performance as the 735.  Yet, I have run at least 8
different codes (real life codes used here) that all out perform the 500 and, 
scaling for clock speeds to the 500X, out perform the 500X too:

  - Reactor acoustic analysis code (HP 2.5 times faster than Alpha 500)
  - Crack Formation calculation code (HP 1.6 times faster)
  - Neutrino Calculation code (HP 1.9 times faster)
  - Health Physics code Case A (HP 5.5 times faster)
  - Health Physics code Case B (HP 3.9 times faster)
  - Groundwater simulation (HP 3.2 times faster)

(Yes, these are vague descriptions, but I wasn't in contact with the authors
directly.  These are however, real life codes.)

Now, these numbers are not meant to incite a flame war, I just need to
give the entire picture of what I'm finding.  I have corresponded with
a few DEC people and they agree with what compiler switches I am using,
but they are suggesting profiling.

This is *exactly* my point.  Just by using regular compiler switches,
I can't reproduce the seemingly resounding defeat of HP by DEC.

Thus, my conclusions are not "DEC is cheating."  They are not "SPEC benchmarks
are useless."  BUT, my conclusions are "What the hell is going on here??"

BTW, the machine configurations:

Alpha 3000/500 (150 MHz)
 - OSF 1.2
 - Patched libm.a to fix exp()_ and sqrt() problems
 - no KAP preprocessor

HP 9000/735 (99 MHz)
 - HP-UX 9.01
 - Regular compiler and library
 - Not using "+OPn" which, I believe enables the KAP preprocessor.

P.S.  I'll probably get flamed for this.
P.P.S. I speak for myself and not for AECL Research.

