well, we've had our Alpha 3400 for almost a week now, so I might as
well get some of the things that have been bugging me about it off my
chest...

1) I was installing some software from CD when suddenly, kernel panic.
No real idea on how to duplicate the bug, but I assume that it was the
CD since I was the only one using the machine at the time, and that
was about all I was doing...

2) Big problems with the utmp file.  Things just don't look right.
User     tty from             login@    idle   JCPU   PCPU what
tjd      p1  unixd2.cis.pitt. 02:34                        w
hahn     :0                   17:07   8554days               -

that's the big one, the :0 thing, though other strange things happen
as well.  Also, xterms never make a utmp entry unless they are a login
shell.  I read the xterm man page, which seems to suggest that the
behaviour I see is correct, but it doesn't seem to work that way
anywhere else!

3) I've had problems with mwm locking up on me a lot.   At least I
assume it's mwm, the mouse still moves, but no windows ever get the
focus, nothing ever happens.  I have to log in from another terminal,
kill it, then log back in.  This happens a couple times a day, for no
apparent reason, and is very annoying when I'm not doing much, and
causes violent reactions when I lose work.

4) a small complaint about the telnet client:  it's a neat idea, being
able to pass environment variables like that, but there should be some
way to mark variables as exported aside from typing 'environ export
FOO' from the telnet prompt.  This doesn't make it very useful for
scripts, now does it?  I hope that I'm wrong about this (and there is
some way to do it), but otherwise, they almost did the Right Thing,
but botched it terribly.

5) the documentation on the bookreader cd is pretty useless, compared
to what I expected, which was OS documentation.

6) .hushlogin doesn't work.

7) The scattering of files all around /etc, /sbin, and /usr/sbin is a
little confusing.  The rc files are kind of a pain too, though that
might just be because I'm not used to it.  What I definately don't
like are all the scripts designed to make setting up the system
easier.  I don't like things changing things without me knowing
exactly what is happening, and it's difficult to figure some things
out without using the scripts.  Some of them are good, I liked the LAT
one and the sendmail one, but it should be easier to set up your
system without using them.

ah well... just a few things off the top of my head, from minor
annoyances to whatever... I'm sure there will be more.   If anyone has
any comments or solutions, I'd be glad to hear about them....

todd.

