In article <1utvjlINNco2@iskut.ucs.ubc.ca> GERTJAN@ERICH.TRIUMF.CA (HOFMAN, GERTJAN) writes:

   Hi There,

   Alpha question....if this is not the right forum or if there
   is a more appropiate one, please tell me.

   We recently got three alpha's (3000) running OS/F  v1.2. to which I
   am trying to port our software from both VMS and DECstations. Right
   now we have 32Mb.
   Is it normal for the DECfortran compiler to  almost freeze the whole
   system ?  While compiling there is almost nothing I can do... my windows
   freeze, typing is sluggish. Is this a memory problem ? All other heavy
   cpu task that I throw at it are handled well,  but F77 makes it die.

This is probably the result of the unified buffer cache (ubc) taking
all the system memory when performing the compile.  You might try
adding the line "ubcmaxpercent 35" to the config file and
re-configuring the kernel, then rebooting.  (35% is just a ballpark
figure that I happen to find useful on my system).  This will prevent
the ubc from taking over all the available vm pages.  It *may* make
your compile go slightly slower, but the system hopefully won't seem
quite so sluggish.

Disclaimer:  This is just a suggestion from a fellow alpha OSF/1 user.
"No warranty is implied."
--
